            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Ashley"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is fantastic!!!! We now use this on everything. Great on sandwiches. Try 1/2 mayo and 1/2 horseradish sauce for a kick."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Awebrie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this for the superbowl along with some twice baked potatos. Everyone loved them. There were none left when the game was over. I followed the suggestions to coat the bacon and lil smokies in brown sugar and marinate in the fridge overnight. They were so good and addicting!\nI mad ethe suggested sauce ut not spicey. Added a lil worchstashire sauce to it. \nNow everytime I see my nephew he asks me to make some more and bring them all to his house. haha\nOH and I did not heed the suggestion of using a roast pan to catch the drippings of fat and was very sad I didnt. Dont ignore that suggestion, youl be glad you did when you go to get the pan out and the jucies DONT go all over the oven making a huge mess!",
            "datePublished": "2010-02-17T15:45:39.560Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "medelamom"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It was a tough one deciding between 4.5 and 5 stars...I usually only give 5 stars if I don't have to tinker with a recipe...but, I couldn't get just half a star to fill in and really, it's just pretty darn good. It was a bit sweet for my taste, so I added a little bit of onion powder (because I LOVE onions, especially with a bbq sauce) and celery salt to cut back the sweetness. That made it just perfect for me. Served with the Sausage Stuffing Balls for supper as we waited for the blizzard to roll in. Loved it!",
            "datePublished": "2011-02-01T13:45:31.997Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Renee"
            },
            "reviewBody": "My daughter is nuts about these...do burn easy so watch the cooking time...really press the brown sugar into the bacon before cooking...lined my baking sheets with foil and clean us was easy...gone first at holiday get togethers.",
            "datePublished": "2012-01-13T10:51:37.157Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SMClanton"
            },
            "reviewBody": "this is soooo good! we used stubbs bourbon bbq sauce and dukes mayo. i think the better the ingredients you use, the better the outcome. they went so good with the brown sugar smokies but would also be so good with numerous things(chicken tenders, fries, on burgers) i can't wait to have another party or get together so i can make this (and the smokies) ;)",
            "datePublished": "2011-10-23T05:34:45.910Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Soup Loving Nicole"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This turned out pretty good. Key is to use a brand of BBQ you are familiar with and love. My go to is a Kansas City based vinegar sauce so the amount of brown sugar was perfect. If using an already sweet sauce, I think you'd need to cut the sugar in half.",
            "datePublished": "2025-02-11T03:12:52.499Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Michael"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We made these last night for a get together with a few neighbors. Did the overnight thing and crisping under the broiler as many have suggested. Frankly, I found the marinade just a sticky mess after overnight. Next time I will do the brown sugar just before the baking. I have done plenty of sugar coated bacon around jalapeno poppers, and they aren't as rock candy hard as these were. I am sure it was the crisping process. Anyway, good recipe, but next time I will stay at 375 and flip 'em during the baking.",
            "datePublished": "2017-11-19T22:35:57.583Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "mom of 4girls"
            },
            "reviewBody": "great recipe.. i did adjust the cooking time a little. I also added extra brown sugar, and put them together the night before and let them soak. Thanks!",
            "datePublished": "2009-05-20T10:12:16.603Z"
          },
          {
